{
 "id": "2018-hagerhall-where",
 "name": "Where Would You Rather Live? Nine Landscapes in Pairs",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Hägerhäll et al. (2018, Frontiers in Psychology 9: 822) tested the long-held claim that people everywhere prefer semi-open landscapes. They rendered one neutral landscape in nine versions, crossing relief (low, medium, high) with vegetation density (sparse, medium, dense), and showed all 36 pairs to 140 people in five indigenous, mostly rural communities (Jahai, Lokono, Makalero, Makasae, Wayuu) and two Swedish student groups, asking each time where they would rather live. Indigenous groups preferred flatter, densely vegetated landscapes; Swedish students preferred the middle picture (medium relief, medium vegetation), as savanna and prospect-refuge theories predict. Half or more of Lokono, Makasae and Jahai participants also preferred the landscape most like the one they grew up in, against under 10% of the Swedish students. This template uses the nine pictures from the paper's Figure 1 (CC BY 4.0) in folder \"landscapes\": all 36 pairs with the question \"Choose the picture that shows where you would rather live\", then a pick of the landscape most like where the participant grew up, plus age, gender, birthplace and current residence as in the paper. Choices are stored per pair, ready for win counts or a Bradley–Terry model with relief and vegetation as factors.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) pairs appear on screen in a new random order with random left/right sides for each participant, instead of laminated A4 sheets in one fixed random order shown by a researcher; (2) the question is the English version of the paper's instruction, not the wording tailored to each language; (3) the pictures are cropped from the published figure (about 330 × 280 px) rather than the original renders; (4) the landscape-you-grew-up-in task shows all nine pictures on one screen in random order.",
 "author": "Hägerhäll, Ode Sang, Englund, Ahlner, Rybka, Huber & Burenhult",
